/**

@page toolbox_particles_memorypool Particle memory pools

\tableofcontents

The particle toolbox normally stores particles as heap objects and moves
pointers through mesh-associated particle sets. This is flexible, but can lead
to memory fragmentation and scattered memory access.

Particle memory pools consolidate particles into larger contiguous chunks. This
reduces allocation overhead and makes particle traversal friendlier to cache and
vectorised kernels. The particle set generator controls which storage flavour is
used for a concrete particle species.

Memory-pool based storage has to preserve the ownership and communication
semantics of vertex-centred particle data. Traversals therefore need gather and
scatter operations when particles are moved into or out of pooled storage.

*/
